<p class="lt-chem-24379"><font color="#000000" face="PT Sans, arial, serif"><span class="mt-font-size-28" style="letter-spacing:1px;line-height:28px;"><b>Summary</b></span></font></p>

<ul>
    <li class="lt-chem-24379">Simple alkanes exist as a homologous series, in which adjacent members differ by a CH<sub>2</sub> unit.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-24379">Condensed chemical formulas show the hydrogen atoms (or other atoms or groups) right next to the carbon atoms to which they are attached.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-24379">Line-angle formulas imply a carbon atom at the corners and ends of lines. Each carbon atom is understood to be attached to enough hydrogen atoms to give each carbon atom four bonds.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-24379">Alkanes have both common names and systematic names, specified by IUPAC.</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-24379">The functional group, a structural arrangement of atoms and/or bonds, is largely responsible for the properties of organic compound families.</li>
</ul>

